  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x2022;	Number of youth trained in VST and entrepreneurship 
&#x2022;	Number of self-help small scale enterprises established through the provision of micro credit facilities</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2014">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x2022;	500 youths trained (2011)
&#x2022;	0 enterprises established with micro-finance support (2011)</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ensure Provision of vocational skills, entrepreneurship training and start-up capital to empower young men and women based on LNOB.With the support of requisite Skills in different fields, mentor-ship and entrepreneurship trainings and start-up capital, the livelihoods of the poor and vulnerable young men and women including people with disabilities shall be improved.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">A.1 60 women were provided with three months training in business start-up and management in areas related to agriculture and animal husbandry. The training was given in cooperation with the Eritrean Women&#x2019;s Association in Agriculture (EWAA).
This training was conducted in Asmara. It covered the following topics: 
?	Poultry and bee production skills
?	Livestock production
?	Fruit and vegetables production
?	Small business administration
?	Resource management
The trained women will be provided with financial and in-kind business start-up assistance in the areas which they choose to engage themselves. 
A.2 Another 60 vulnerable women with socio-economic difficulties and women affected by the impacts of HIV/AIDS residing in the Town of Barentu were provided with training in sewing/tailoring while 30 others are being trained in basic computer applications.
A.3 A batch of 40 women graduated from a training in sewing/tailoring in the Tokombia training centre of NCEW. Other 50 women are undergoing training in various handicrafts such as mat-making and beaded beautification artifacts making.,
A.4 A group of other 60 women in Massawa were trained in basic computer application in the facilities of the Red Sea General Construction and Development Company.
A.5 In Massawa, 60 women are undergoing three-months training in modern cooking (food preparation) 
A.6 A total of 150 women workers selected from different enterprises have been given various educational seminars in leadership, management and gender issues with the aim of empowering them to accede competitively and competently to different hierarchies of decision-making processes in the workers&#x2019; organizations, through impacting them with knowledge and skills which will help them steer themselves through the trade union and gender matters affecting them routinely.
Among them, 60 were in zoba Maekel, 30 in Northern red Sea and 20 each in zoba Anseba, Debub and Gash Barka regions. They were also given courses in training of trainers so that they would be enabled to train their peers at work. 
From the total number of beneficiaries 60% are women and 70% are youth.

B.	Economic support
B.1 In partnership with Anseba Regional Administration, the NCEW identified a group of vulnerable peasant women residing in the sub-zoba of Haboro and agreed to assist them in the leveling of their farmlands, as well as repairing a diversion irrigation canal which was damaged due to heavy floods. The NCEW has signed a contract with Segen construction company to undertake the works.
B.2 Some 150 vulnerable women from seven (7) villages in the sub-zone of Laelay Gash, in Gash Barka region, were assisted with Nakfa 30,000 (Thirty thousand) worth asset building assistance to enable them rebuild their livelihoods by engaging in horticulture production, trade, reproduction of livestock, bee-keeping and animal driven carts.
B.3 A group of 80 Eritrean women from among those who recently returned from refuge in Ethiopia and resettled among communities in the Gash Barka region were assisted with Nakfa 10,000 (Ten thousand) each to help them become reintegrated economically.
B.3 Thirty (30) women who received trainings in livestock production and management of small businesses were provided with Nakfa 50,000 (Fifty thousand) each in-kind and cash business startup support to invest according to their choice of activities which they presented to the NCEW.
B.4 Women who were trained in weaving last year (2021) through the UNDP supported joint project of the three national associations could not make meaningful incomes out of the skills they acquired through the training. This is because of the impacts of the COVID induced lockdown, as well as inability to sell their products profitably because the market was flooded with cheaper imported clothes. 
The NCEW in collaboration with the NUEW and the administration of zoba Maekel is making efforts to help them find a place where they can work and sell their products a group of cooperative. 
B.5 The NCEW has initiated a procurement process to buy 20 sewing machines to be provided to the women who were trained in sewing last year in Barentu, Gash Barka Region, through the UNDP supported project. Discussions are also underway to provide similar support to women trained in Tokombia.
B.6 Five farmer women&#x2019;s cooperatives (each comprising 10-15 women) in Tokombia have been given training in irrigated farming. Water wells to help them to produce year-round irrigated and rain-fed crops are being dug, with almost 62% of the works completed. The procurement of water pumps, irrigation pipes and other implements will be carried out in near future.
B.7 Based on lessons learnt from last year&#x2019;s experiences, purchase order for the fabrication of 20 shoe-shine stands was placed with Segen construction company. Already 50% of the work is completed. Identification of beneficiaries has started in collaboration with the zoba Maekel Regional Administration and the NUEW.
B.8 Fifty (50) women who lost their jobs due to various reasons, including the impacts of COVID-19 pandemic were given temporary livelihood problem alleviation support ranging from Nakfa 5000 to Nakfa 10,000 each.
B.19 Building upon the experiences of the UNDP supported distribution of COVID-19 prevention sanitation materials in 2020 and 2021, the NCEW has made efforts to strengthen the workplace safety and health programmes through the provision of more durable and multi-user hand-washing water stands. It has placed a fabrication order for the procurement of 50 water stands with 6 faucets for as many users to be able to utilize it at one time.
B.20 The bald of the budget was spend to the procurement on other non-financial support takes place to enhance the capacity and create income for disadvantageous women&#x2019;s in :-
1. 600 Bee hive boxes for 300 families, 
2. 150 Donkeys with water tanker (jerikan)  
3. 1500 Sheep/Goat for 100 families
From the total number of beneficiaries 65% are women and 60% are youth.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">. Support poor and vulnerable youth in vocational and entrepreneurship skills training
. Strengthening Civil Society Organizations
. Conduct TOT in different vocational skills
. Provide start up capital to improve their livelihoods</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP strengthened the National Union of Eritrean Youth and Students; the National Confederation of Eritrean Workers, and the National Union of Eritrean Women capacities to support sustainable youth employment opportunities directly and indirectly. Through UNDP financial and technical support, these organizations are contributing on enhancing productive capacities of youth through provision of technical, financial, and productive assets. This year 640 beneficiaries have received modern beehives (two beehive each), 380 households received a total of 9,500 chicks and materials for feeding and watering plus six months&#x2019; supply of cheek feed, 55 households received 14 heads of goats each. The UNDP support will help them improve their livelihoods through alternative income generation schemes. To safeguard informal workers&#x2019; livelihoods from the disruptive effects of COVID-19: 30 women who rendered jobless due to the closure of business as a result of the COVID-19 were given with livelihood support of Nakfa 3,000.00 each; 3 youths in Asmara and 2 women in Keren town were rehabilitated through the provision of shoeshine stations; 3 vulnerable households in Keren and other 5 households in the Northern Red Sea Region have been given cash and in-kind assistance to establish small mercantile businesses and 50 women heads of household residing in and around Tokombia were given 10 female goats each to assist them rebuild back their livestock assets and livelihoods. The above supports and initiatives have enhanced the productive capacity of the households by diversifying and/or rebuilding back their productive base and assets (CPD Indicators 1.1.1 and 1.1.2). 
With the support of UNDP, in Anseba region, Adi-Teklezan sub-region, 20 young women and women headed households&#x2019; beneficiaries acquired skills in bee keeping and another 20 in basic computer. 19 beneficiaries have graduated from the ceramic craftsmanship skills and an additional 13 young women and 2 young men graduated in basic computer skills, 50 women acquired hair dressing and 12 young women and men acquired skills in different musical instruments. In Asmara, 245 youth acquired Video production skills and 45 in basic computer application. In addition, attaching with different establishments 51 females have acquired driving skills training given by Segen Construction Company, 30 poor and vulnerable youth women acquired skills in tourism/hospitality by Tourism Training Center. UNDP supported in strengthening the National Confederation of Eritrean Workers Training Center, which is functioning in seven Departments namely electronics, computer networking, electricity, basic computer application, air condition and refrigeration, wood works, and metal works. The training center has received accreditation from the Ministry of Education recently. This year refresher courses were provided to students enrolled in the Training Center prior to receiving its accreditation were brought together for a refresher course offered for 99 in basic electronics, 40 in electricity, 30 in basic computer, 18 in networking, 45 in air condition and refrigeration, 29 in wood and metal works.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">. Support poor and vulnerable youth in vocational and entrepreneurship skills training
. Conduct TOT in different vocational skills
. Provide start up capital to improve their livelihoods</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">With the support of UNDP, 604 poor and vulnerable youth, of which about 58% are women and girls have acquired skills and knowledge on management and leadership, secretarial science, media production, advanced computer technology, Arabic language, music and drama/theater, electronics, Training of Trainers on vocational skills and entrepreneurship, tourism and customer service, advanced networking, accounting, and others. The selected institutions have recruited trainers and enhanced well their training centers. The project is supporting the youths, including women and people with disabilities, to acquire requisite vocational skills and entrepreneurship capabilities; and to provide business start-up financing for micro, small and medium scale enterprises. 
With its financial and technical support, UNDP is strengthening the National Union of Eritrean Youth and Students; the National Confederation of Eritrean Workers, and the National Union of Eritrean Women which directly and indirectly support sustainable youth employment opportunities. Through UNDP support, these organizations are contributing on enhancing productive capacities of youth through provision of technical and productive assets. 600 poor and vulnerable household farmers have received beehives (one beehive each) to help them improve their livelihoods through generation of alternative income from bee keeping business. There is also a plan to further diversify production and promotion of alternative sources of income generating livelihood opportunities through a package of inter-related economic activities and a community-oriented approach to make a difference in the livelihoods of poor young people.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
